"executive_summary": "This study presents an analysis of three-particle azimuthal correlations in proton-proton (pp), deuteron-gold (d+Au), and gold-gold (Au+Au) collisions at a center-of-mass energy per nucleon pair of 200 GeV, utilizing data from the STAR experiment. The research focuses on identifying signatures of Mach cone shock waves generated by high-energy partons traversing the quark-gluon plasma (QGP). By analyzing correlations between a high-momentum trigger particle (3 < pT < 4 GeV/c) and two softer associated particles (1 < pT < 2 GeV/c), the authors investigate the away-side structure of jet-like correlations. The analysis employs a rigorous background subtraction method to isolate genuine three-particle signals from combinatorial backgrounds arising from flow harmonics and uncorrelated particle production. Key findings include the observation of off-diagonal peaks in central Au+Au collisions at an angle of approximately 1.45 radians relative to the trigger, which is consistent with conical emission. Furthermore, the study demonstrates that the emission angle remains constant across different associated particle transverse momenta, a result that distinguishes hydrodynamic Mach cone emission from alternative mechanisms like QCD-Cherenkov radiation, which would predict a momentum-dependent angle. The robustness of the signal against variations in flow harmonic measurements and normalization windows confirms the reliability of the observation, suggesting the presence of Mach cones in the heavy ion collision medium.",
